NEX patch for go-gost/x v0.8.1, applied by gost-runtime/build.sh after nex-service-close.patch.

A chain that has nodes never dials outside the chain because its selector came up empty (nex6).

Root cause. A hop with two or more nodes and a selector runs the fail filter first
(selector/filter.go): a node whose fail marker reached maxFails less than failTimeout ago is
dropped. When every node of the hop is inside its fail window the filter returned an EMPTY list,
so defaultSelector.Select returned nil (selector/selector.go), chainHop.Select returned nil
(hop/hop.go), and Chain.Route stopped there and returned the route built so far (chain/chain.go):
  - single hop: a route with no nodes, which chainRoute.Dial hands to DefaultRoute.Dial - a plain
    dial of the target from this host (chain/route.go);
  - later hop of a multi-hop chain: a route that ends at the previous hop, so that relay is asked
    to connect to the target itself.
In both cases the service's forwarder target is reached WITHOUT the tunnel exit. NEX writes
exactly such hops for exit groups and parallel relays (maxFails 1, failTimeout 5s / 15s, the
entry's forwarder target is the rule's real target): once all exits had failed, every new TCP
connection and UDP session left through the entry host (or the relay host) for the next 5-15 s.
The same nil is what hop.Select returns on purpose when a bypass matches, which is why upstream
goes direct on it.

Fix, two layers:
  1. selector/filter.go - failFilter.Filter never returns an empty list: when every object is
     marked failed it returns all of them (try them anyway, as nginx does when all upstream
     servers are down). The connection then fails on a dead exit instead of leaving the tunnel,
     and an exit that came back is used at once instead of after failTimeout. This also covers
     chain groups (FailFilter[chain.Chainer]) and forwarder node selectors.
  2. hop/hop.go, registry/hop.go, chain/chain.go, chain/route.go - defence in depth. chainHop
     (and the registry wrapper) gain NexSelect, which reports WHY no node came back:
     direct == true for the deliberate cases (hop-level bypass matched; no eligible node - empty
     hop, or every node excluded by its own bypass / matcher / filter), false when eligible nodes
     exist but the selector selected none. Chain.Route keeps the upstream behaviour for the
     deliberate cases and returns a route whose Dial / Bind fail with ErrNexNoNode for the other,
     so no selector or filter (present or future, built-in or custom) can turn "all nodes
     unavailable" into a direct dial. Hop.Select itself is unchanged for its other callers
     (forwarders); plugin hops, which cannot report the reason, behave as upstream.

Regression tests: selector/nex_noempty_test.go, chain/nex_noroute_test.go (go test ./selector/
./chain/ in the patched go-gost/x tree). End to end with real processes, TCP and UDP, single and
multi hop, and the bypass paths that must stay direct: gost-runtime/test/bypass-check/run.sh.

